# Pre-push guards, then ONE scope-gated local check. git pipes the pushed-ref
# list on stdin; capture it ONCE and feed all three checks (stdin can only be
# read once). Bypass intentionally with `git push --no-verify`.
#   1. guard-protected-push  — refuse force-push / deletion of main (#163).
#   2. guard-commit-subjects — reject any pushed commit whose subject violates
#      the Conventional-Commits rule, even if commit-msg was bypassed
#      (--no-verify / worktree hook couldn't spawn). Backstops the @-leak that
#      shipped on #856.
#   3. is-docs-only-push     — skip the local check entirely when every
#      changed file is docs (same test as CONTRIBUTING.md's CI doc-only
#      fast-path) — a docs-only diff has no runtime surface to exercise.
#
# ops-2997 (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-05-commit-gate-rebalance-design.md,
# Part 1): this hook no longer runs `verify:fast:branch`'s ~35-minute battery
# — a healthy run cost that on its own arithmetic, and under concurrency it
# degraded to hours-long deadlock. CI (`npm run verify`, a required status
# check on `main`) is the enforcing gate now; local hooks only inform. The
# ONE exception is `test:sidecar`, scope-gated to `server/tts-sidecar/**`: it
# spawns pytest, not a vitest fork pool, so it doesn't violate "no hook may
# spawn a process pool," and it's the only automatic executor of the
# `pytest.importorskip("torch")` tests CI deliberately can't run (its own
# lean sidecar install skips torch, see verify.yml). Costs ~6.85 min on a
# sidecar-touching push and near-zero when the step is out of scope or its
# input hash is already cached — but NOT literally "zero on every other push":
# a root package.json / package-lock.json / .github/actions/** change trips
# computeShared in verify-cache.mjs, which puts every step in scope whatever
# its own globs say, so that push pays the full cost on a cold cache.
#
# ops-71 (#3047, Part 3 of the same design, "A reaper that actually runs"):
# every push also runs the stale-battery census — ONE Win32_Process query,
# ~300ms, no pool — which appends a log entry (every root's command line
# included, the dataset the deferred concurrency-governor question needs)
# and kills only PROVABLY orphaned trees (a dead parent, per classify()'s
# reachability test). It never blocks the push: reap-stale-batteries.mjs's
# own CLI catches everything and always exits 0 (see runCli's try/catch and
# its "always returns 0, even when the census throws" test).
#
# Deliberately placed BEFORE the is-docs-only-push branch, so it runs on
# EVERY push, docs-only included: the census costs ~300ms and spawns no
# pool, so a docs-only push isn't cheapened by skipping it, and a docs-only
# push is exactly as likely as any other to be the one that happens to run
# after an earlier battery orphaned a subtree — the orphan doesn't know or
# care what kind of diff is being pushed next. Skipping the census on
# docs-only pushes would just mean orphans from an all-docs stretch of work
# never get swept until a code push finally happens along.
PUSH_REFS=$(cat)
printf '%s\n' "$PUSH_REFS" | node scripts/guard-protected-push.mjs "$@" || exit 1
printf '%s\n' "$PUSH_REFS" | node scripts/guard-commit-subjects.mjs "$@" || exit 1
node scripts/reap-stale-batteries.mjs --pre-push
if printf '%s\n' "$PUSH_REFS" | node scripts/is-docs-only-push.mjs "$@"; then
  exit 0
fi
node scripts/verify-cache.mjs --steps test:sidecar --scope-branch
